WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.315
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.615 --> 00:00:08.874
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.174 --> 00:00:18.084
Unit thirty-five: Ageing Societies. In this unit, you will discuss the challenges and opportunities of ageing populations.

4
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:20.514
ageing population.

5
00:00:21.214 --> 00:00:25.803
ageing population means a population with a growing proportion of older people.

6
00:00:26.103 --> 00:00:29.414
Japan has one of the world's oldest ageing populations.

7
00:00:30.114 --> 00:00:33.649
Your turn. Say: ageing population.

8
00:00:36.349 --> 00:00:37.803
ageing population.

9
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:40.329
dependency ratio.

10
00:00:41.029 --> 00:00:46.755
dependency ratio means the number of non-working people compared to working people.

11
00:00:47.055 --> 00:00:50.424
A rising dependency ratio puts pressure on taxes.

12
00:00:51.124 --> 00:00:54.404
Your turn. Say: dependency ratio.

13
00:00:57.104 --> 00:00:58.558
dependency ratio.

14
00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:00.969
retirement age.

15
00:01:01.669 --> 00:01:06.919
retirement age means the age at which people usually stop working.

16
00:01:07.219 --> 00:01:10.310
Several countries are raising the retirement age.

17
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.092
Your turn. Say: retirement age.

18
00:01:16.792 --> 00:01:18.200
retirement age.

19
00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:20.486
pension crisis.

20
00:01:21.186 --> 00:01:26.564
pension crisis means a situation where pension funds cannot pay future retirees.

21
00:01:26.864 --> 00:01:29.688
Economists warn of a coming pension crisis.

22
00:01:30.388 --> 00:01:33.203
Your turn. Say: pension crisis.

23
00:01:35.903 --> 00:01:37.252
pension crisis.

24
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.414
elder care.

25
00:01:40.114 --> 00:01:43.181
elder care means care and support for older people.

26
00:01:43.481 --> 00:01:45.910
Demand for elder care is rising quickly.

27
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.692
Your turn. Say: elder care.

28
00:01:52.392 --> 00:01:53.428
elder care.

29
00:01:54.417 --> 00:01:55.720
longevity.

30
00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:58.489
longevity means long life.

31
00:01:58.789 --> 00:02:01.462
Healthy diets contribute to longevity.

32
00:02:02.162 --> 00:02:05.371
Your turn. Say: longevity.

33
00:02:08.071 --> 00:02:09.247
longevity.

34
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.517
intergenerational solidarity.

35
00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:19.431
intergenerational solidarity means support and cooperation between young and old.

36
00:02:19.731 --> 00:02:24.691
Family meals encourage intergenerational solidarity.

37
00:02:25.391 --> 00:02:29.552
Your turn. Say: intergenerational solidarity.

38
00:02:32.252 --> 00:02:34.391
intergenerational solidarity.

39
00:02:35.383 --> 00:02:36.628
silver economy.

40
00:02:37.328 --> 00:02:41.685
silver economy means products and services designed for older people.

41
00:02:41.985 --> 00:02:45.726
The silver economy creates new business opportunities.

42
00:02:46.426 --> 00:02:49.844
Your turn. Say: silver economy.

43
00:02:52.544 --> 00:02:53.975
silver economy.

44
00:02:54.983 --> 00:02:57.308
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:57.608 --> 00:03:02.313
Rithy, did you know Cambodia is one of the youngest countries in Asia?

46
00:03:03.617 --> 00:03:08.008
Yes, but a report I read warned that this would change within a few decades.

47
00:03:09.317 --> 00:03:14.454
It will. Japan and Korea have already reached very high dependency ratios.

48
00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:20.025
Economists there admitted having underestimated the pension crisis.

49
00:03:21.317 --> 00:03:25.011
And governments have urged citizens to save more for retirement.

50
00:03:26.317 --> 00:03:31.312
My grandmother insists that families, not governments, should care for the elderly.

51
00:03:32.617 --> 00:03:37.730
That's a strong tradition here. Intergenerational solidarity is valuable.

52
00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:44.197
Still, as cities grow, professional elder care will be needed too.

53
00:03:45.483 --> 00:03:49.770
Grammar focus: Reported speech with nuance (extended).

54
00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.045
Reporting verbs carry meaning about attitude.

55
00:03:53.345 --> 00:03:59.619
A report warned that this would change. Economists admitted having underestimated the crisis.

56
00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:03.986
Concede suggests reluctance, and claim suggests some doubt.

57
00:04:04.286 --> 00:04:11.858
Choose reporting verbs carefully: 'concede' suggests reluctance, 'claim' suggests doubt, 'reveal' suggests surprise.

58
00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:15.804
Now read and listen. The Grey Revolution.

59
00:04:16.104 --> 00:04:19.416
People around the world are living longer than ever before.

60
00:04:20.717 --> 00:04:25.851
Longevity is a remarkable achievement, but it also creates economic challenges.

61
00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:32.133
In many developed countries, the dependency ratio is rising rapidly.

62
00:04:33.417 --> 00:04:37.750
Governments have warned that pension systems may become unsustainable.

63
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.290
Several have raised the retirement age, despite public protests.

64
00:04:44.583 --> 00:04:49.160
Demand for elder care is growing, while fewer young workers are available.

65
00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:57.430
At the same time, the silver economy is creating new markets in health, tourism and technology.

66
00:04:58.717 --> 00:05:04.188
Experts insist that intergenerational solidarity will be essential for the future.

67
00:05:05.483 --> 00:05:08.345
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:08.645 --> 00:05:13.059
The number of non-working people compared to workers is the blank.

69
00:05:13.359 --> 00:05:19.300
A, dependency ratio. B, retirement age. C, silver economy.

70
00:05:23.283 --> 00:05:26.200
The answer is A: dependency ratio.

71
00:05:27.717 --> 00:05:30.181
The minister blank that taxes might rise.

72
00:05:30.481 --> 00:05:34.587
A, warned. B, told. C, said us.

73
00:05:38.583 --> 00:05:40.374
The answer is A: warned.

74
00:05:41.883 --> 00:05:43.732
He admitted blank the mistake.

75
00:05:44.032 --> 00:05:47.732
A, to make. B, having made. C, make.

76
00:05:51.717 --> 00:05:54.413
The answer is B: having made.

77
00:05:55.917 --> 00:05:57.510
Long life is blank.

78
00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:02.218
A, longevity. B, elder care. C, pension crisis.

79
00:06:06.217 --> 00:06:09.017
The answer is A: longevity.

80
00:06:10.517 --> 00:06:14.211
According to the text, what is the silver economy creating?

81
00:06:14.511 --> 00:06:19.302
A, new markets. B, higher taxes. C, fewer jobs.

82
00:06:23.317 --> 00:06:26.245
The answer is A: new markets.

83
00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:33.154
Now it's your turn. Part one: Who should be responsible for caring for older people?

84
00:06:38.454 --> 00:06:44.285
Part two: Report one view you disagree with, then give your position and two reasons.

85
00:06:50.585 --> 00:07:02.850
Some people insist that families alone should care for the elderly. I'd concede that family care is valuable, but I believe the state must help too, because families are smaller and many young people work far from home.

86
00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.881
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:06.883 --> 00:07:12.995
Great work today! You learned eight new words and reported speech with nuance (extended).

88
00:07:13.295 --> 00:07:16.621
Today's value is kindness. Be kind to everyone.

89
00:07:16.921 --> 00:07:20.526
See you in Unit thirty-six. Goodbye!

90
00:07:20.826 --> 00:07:23.989
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

